NAME name;

char course[20];

DATE DOB;

float fees;
} student;
In this example, we see that the structure student contains two other structures, NAME and DATE. Both 
these structures have their own fields. The structure NAME has three fields: first_name, mid_name, and 
last_name. The structure DATE also has three fields: dd, mm, and yy, which specify the day, month, 
and year of the date. Now, to assign values to the structure fields, we will write
student stud1;
stud1.r_no = 01;
stud1.name.first_name = "Janak";
stud1.name.mid_name = "Raj";
stud1.name.last_name = "Thareja";
stud1.course = "BCA";
stud1.DOB.dd = 15;
stud1.DOB.mm = 09;
stud1.DOB.yy = 1990;
stud1.fees = 45000;
In case of nested structures, we use the dot operator in conjunction with the structure variables 
to access the members of the innermost as well as the outermost structures. The use of nested 
structures is illustrated in the next program.
