Evaluation of a Postfix Expression
The ease of evaluation acts as the driving force for computers to translate an infix notation into 
a postfix notation. That is, given an algebraic expression written in infix notation, the computer 
first converts the expression into the equivalent postfix notation and then evaluates the postfix 
expression.

Both these tasks—converting the infix notation into postfix notation and evaluating the postfix 
expression—make extensive use of stacks as the primary tool.

Using stacks, any postfix expression can be evaluated very easily. Every character of the postfix 
expression is scanned from left to right. If the character encountered is an operand, it is pushed 
on to the stack. However, if an operator is encountered, then the top two values are popped from 
the stack and the operator is applied on these values. The result is then pushed on to the stack. 
Let us look at Fig. 7.23 which shows the algorithm to evaluate a postfix expression.

